@meta
id: 01-13
title: A look at visual mode
chapter: 01
type: lesson
skills: mode-awareness, esc, insert-a, survival-motion
par: 19
minutes: 4
vscode: click and drag, or Shift with the arrow keys
open: totals.csv

@brief
You have been in four modes now: normal, insert, command line and
replace. Here is the fifth, briefly, so that the map is complete.

`v` puts you in VISUAL mode. The character under the cursor is selected,
and every motion you make extends the selection instead of just moving.
It is the closest thing vim has to dragging with a mouse, and it exists
for the times when it is easier to see what you are about to change than
to describe it.

Chapter 22 is where visual mode does any work, because the work needs
verbs you have not been given yet. Today, just visit.

  1. Put the cursor on the last row and press `v`, then `$`. Watch the
     row highlight and the indicator read `-- VISUAL --`.
  2. Press `Esc`. Nothing has changed — visual mode on its own is a
     selection, not an edit.
  3. The total on that row is cut off. It should read `12.50`.

@keys
| `v` | visual mode, character by character |
| `V` | visual mode, whole lines |
| `Esc` | drop the selection, change nothing |
| `$` `a` | end of the line, append |

@hints
`jj` gets you to the `ord_1043` row. `v` starts the selection there and
`$` drags it to the end of the line.
--
`Esc` collapses the selection. The cursor is left where the selection
ended, which is a genuinely useful side effect once you know it.
--
The row ends `12.` and wants two more digits. `$a50` then `Esc`, then
save and quit.

@solution
    j j v $ <Esc> $ a 5 0 <Esc> :wq<Enter>

The reason visual mode is late in this curriculum rather than early is
that it is a trap for people coming from VS Code. It is familiar, so it
becomes the thing you reach for, and you end up using vim as a slower
mouse: select, then act, select, then act.

The faster habit is to say what you mean in one go — delete this word,
change what is inside these brackets — which is what chapters 8 to 10
build. Visual mode stays in the toolbox for the genuinely awkward cases
where the region is easier to see than to name, and there are plenty of
those. It is just not the default.

@start totals.csv
order_id,customer,total
ord_1042,priya,48.00
ord_1043,marco,12.

@expected totals.csv
order_id,customer,total
ord_1042,priya,48.00
ord_1043,marco,12.50
